course: dictionary
lesson: 10115
title: Old words, old places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like they belong in a storybook or an old book. Thither is one of them. It means "to that place" or "there". «He went thither on foot» means he walked to that place.
explain: Old words often sound grand or formal, and writers use them to make a text feel old. Hither means to here, and whither means to where, so thither is part of a small family of old words that match each other.
explain: You still hear one of these today in «The leaves blew hither and thither.» Hither and thither means here and there, in many directions. The leaves are not going to one place, so the context tells you the meaning is "all over the place".
fact: Thither is an adverb, and it is old-fashioned or formal.
fact: «Thither» means to that place or there.
fact: «Hither and thither» means here and there, in many directions.
fact: The family: hither means to here, thither means to there, and whither means to where?
fact: When you meet an old word, notice how the text sounds. The grand tone is a clue that the writer is using old language on purpose.
exercise: «He went thither on foot.» What does thither mean here? | To the sea | Under the table | Quickly and happily | *To that place
exercise: «The leaves blew hither and thither.» What does hither and thither mean? | Very fast in one direction | Quietly and slowly | Under the trees | *Here and there, in many directions
exercise: Which old word means "to here"? | whither | hence | *hither | thither
test: «She walked thither across the field.» Where did she walk? | *To that place | Very slowly and carefully | Across the sea | To this place, right beside her
test: Which old word asks the question "to where?" | hither | hence | *whither | thither
exam: Jayden asks, "Is thither a modern word?" | It is a slang word from the internet | It means "to here" | It is only used in Scotland | *It is old-fashioned, and it mostly survives in "hither and thither"
